{#- modules/guardrails/control-profile.j2
    One slide shape, filled in for every control the audience might reach for.

    Context files, hooks, sandboxing, gateways, human
    review, agent review — each gets the same three bands in the same places,
    so the slides can be flipped through and compared rather than heard as six
    consecutive tool pitches. The point is not any single profile: it is that
    nothing comes out green across the board, and the *shape* of the tradeoff
    differs per control.

    ── The three bands ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    1. Name + one line of what it actually is.
    2. Benefits / Shortcomings — two columns, each item a title plus one
       sentence. Never a restatement of the bars underneath: the bars score,
       these explain. Most of this copy is lifted verbatim from slides that
       already existed elsewhere in the repo (guardrail-decisions.j2 for hooks
       and agent review, tool-calls.j2 for sandboxing, the coverage() matrix
       for human review); gateways and the shortcoming halves for sandboxing
       and human review had no source and are drafted here.
    3. The six bars, full width, no per-bar notes and no caption, with optional
       footnotes under them for what a 1-to-4 score cannot carry — where the
       score is heading, or what it hides. The columns
       above already said it in words; a third telling would just crowd the
       slide. With no notes, meter_row drops its note column and the bars span
       the same width as the two columns above them.

    "Repeatable" is 4 when the control gives the same answer every time it
    runs. It is the axis that separates a script from a model, and it is why
    hooks and agent review — similar on every other bar — sit at opposite ends.

    Scoring convention, and the only rule that matters here:
    **4 is always the favourable end.** Not "high", not "a lot" — favourable.
    So cheap scores 4 on Cost, and fast feedback scores 4 on Speed. If a bar
    were ever read as raw magnitude, the whole slide would flip meaning
    halfway down and the comparison would be worthless.

    Replaces the older control-scorecard.j2, which was bars only.

    Written against the brand-pack primitive contract (see AGENTS.md): no hex
    colors, no brand class names. Import WITH CONTEXT:

      {%- from "modules/guardrails/control-profile.j2" import
          context_files, hooks, sandboxing, llm_gateway, human_review,
          agent_review with context %}
-#}

{%- from "modules/branding/" ~ branding ~ "/primitives.j2" import
    content_slide,
    slide_heading,
    pros_cons,
    meter_row
-%}

{%- macro _profile(name, tagline, good, avoid, scores, good_label="Good when", avoid_label="Avoid when", nav="", notes=[]) -%}
{#- The shared shape. The per-control macros below are data, not markup, so a
    new control is one dict away and lands in the same layout automatically.
    The dimension order is fixed for every control — reordering it for one
    slide would break the flip-through comparison, which is the whole idea. -#}
{%- call content_slide(nav or name) %}
  {{ slide_heading("", name, tagline) }}
  {#- Two beats, one spot. The columns are the slide when it opens; on the next
      press they are REPLACED in place by the bars. Both blocks share a single
      grid cell (`.swap-stack`), so the cell is as tall as the columns and
      nothing else on the slide moves when they trade places. `.swap-stack`,
      `.swap-out` and `.step` are engine classes (see modules/shared/
      modern_template.j2), not brand ones. -#}
  <div class="swap-stack">
    <div class="swap-out">
      {{ pros_cons(good, avoid, good_label, avoid_label) }}
    </div>
    <div class="step" data-step="profile-bars">
      {#- Only rendered in the PDF, where this half becomes its own page and
          would otherwise arrive with no title on it. -#}
      <div class="print-only">{{ slide_heading("", name, tagline) }}</div>
      {{ meter_row([
           {"label": "Setup",         "score": scores.setup},
           {"label": "Maintenance",   "score": scores.maintenance},
           {"label": "Cost",          "score": scores.cost},
           {"label": "Speed",         "score": scores.speed},
           {"label": "Effectiveness", "score": scores.effectiveness},
           {"label": "Repeatable",    "score": scores.repeatable},
         ]) }}
      {%- if notes %}
      {#- Footnotes to the bars: the things a 1-to-4 score cannot say. A score
          is a snapshot; these are where it is heading and what it hides. -#}
      <ul style="margin:22px 0 0 34px; padding:0; list-style:none; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:8px;">
        {%- for note in notes %}
        <li style="font-size:15px; line-height:1.5; color:var(--slide-ink-2);">{{ note|safe }}</li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ul>
      {%- endif %}
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro context_files() -%}
{#- The cheapest thing on the list and the weakest control on the list. Worth
    saying out loud: a file the model may or may not follow is guidance, not
    enforcement — which is exactly why it scores well on setup and speed and
    then bottoms out on effectiveness and repeatability. Cost is 3 rather than
    4: the file is free, but it spends context-window budget on every single
    session, which is the tradeoff the degradation slide just made visible.

    Its columns are the long-standing tradeoff list for context files. -#}
{{ _profile(
   "Context files",
   "Rules and conventions the agent reads before it writes",
   [{"title": "Very little cost",
     "detail": "Just a markdown file &mdash; though it does add tokens to every session."},
    {"title": "Version-controlled",
     "detail": "Reviewed and merged like any other code change."},
    {"title": "Works everywhere the agent runs",
     "detail": "One file, picked up by IDE agents, CLI agents, and CI reviewers alike."},
    {"title": "Reduces variability",
     "detail": "Across developers and agents &mdash; &ldquo;we do it this way here.&rdquo;"}],
   [{"title": "Doesn&rsquo;t scale",
     "detail": "One file per repo &mdash; hundreds of files to update for one policy change."},
    {"title": "Hard to maintain",
     "detail": "Projects evolve and files become stale misinformation."},
    {"title": "Best-effort instructions",
     "detail": "The agent may downweight or disregard them. No real enforcement."},
    {"title": "No audit trail",
     "detail": "No evidence of which rule applied where &mdash; bad for compliance."}],
   {"setup": 4, "maintenance": 2, "cost": 3, "speed": 4, "effectiveness": 1, "repeatable": 1},
   good_label="Benefits", avoid_label="Shortcomings",
   notes=[
     "More effective as models get smarter and context windows grow.",
     "Cost starts cheap but can devolve into expensive misinformation.",
   ]) }}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro hooks() -%}
{#- Deterministic and blocking, inside the loop, at almost no cost. The catch
    is scope and authorship: it only binds the agent that honours it, and the
    languages that do this well are ones almost nobody writes fluently — which
    is why Setup and Maintenance both score 1. Writing the rule is the hard
    part, and the rule is never finished.

    The first two shortcomings are lifted from should_you_hooks() in
    guardrail-decisions.j2; the benefits and the third shortcoming are Jon's. -#}
{{ _profile(
   "Hooks",
   "Deterministic checks on what the agent is about to do",
   [{"title": "Only adds context when useful",
     "detail": "If the hook runs and finds nothing, it does not waste any tokens."},
    {"title": "Static analysis is deterministic",
     "detail": "Write custom rules for the tools you already own &mdash; Semgrep, Snyk, and the rest."},
    {"title": "Can evidence a negative",
     "detail": "You can show that at a point in time, in a given scenario, it absolutely did not have a specific issue."}],
   [{"title": "Local, so trivially bypassed",
     "detail": "It runs on a machine you do not control. Never count a hook as your enforcement point."},
    {"title": "Latency is a hard budget",
     "detail": "Past a couple of seconds people disable it. A slow hook is a control with a countdown timer."},
    {"title": "Not flexible, and hard to write",
     "detail": "The best-equipped languages are very specialised, and even frontier models are bad at writing them. And they have bugs and miss corner cases, like all software."}],
   {"setup": 1, "maintenance": 1, "cost": 4, "speed": 4, "effectiveness": 3, "repeatable": 4},
   good_label="Benefits", avoid_label="Shortcomings",
   nav="Profile: Hooks") }}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro sandboxing() -%}
{#- The odd one out: it does not judge the change at all, it bounds what the
    agent can reach while making it. Benefits are lifted from
    sandboxing_nono() in modules/governance/tool-calls.j2; the shortcomings had
    no source anywhere in the repo and are drafted here, grounded in that
    slide's own callout and this deck's NOTES.md. -#}
{{ _profile(
   "Sandboxing",
   "Bounding what the agent can reach, rather than judging what it wrote",
   [{"title": "Enforced below the agent",
     "detail": "The kernel, the hypervisor, the platform &mdash; something it cannot talk its way past. Not its own cooperation."},
    {"title": "Cheap enough to always be on",
     "detail": "No rules to write and none to maintain. The sandbox nobody turns off is the one that protects you."},
    {"title": "Deny by default",
     "detail": "File access, network reach and credentials start at nothing and are granted per task, not per install."}],
   [{"title": "Slows users down",
     "detail": "Prompts, denials and re-runs all land on the person. The tighter the box, the more often the work stops to ask."},
    {"title": "Too tight and it gets removed",
     "detail": "A sandbox that blocks legitimate work does not get loosened, it gets switched off &mdash; and then you have nothing."},
    {"title": "Tells you it stopped, not what was wrong",
     "detail": "A denied call is not a finding. You learn there was a problem, never which one, and there is nothing to hand a reviewer."}],
   {"setup": 1, "maintenance": 1, "cost": 4, "speed": 4, "effectiveness": 3, "repeatable": 4},
   good_label="Benefits", avoid_label="Shortcomings") }}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro llm_gateway() -%}
{#- Expensive to stand up and to run, and the only control here that sees every
    call regardless of which client made it. That chokepoint is what you are
    buying; the infrastructure is what you are paying. Both columns are drafted
    from the per-dimension notes in the retired control-scorecard.j2 (git
    1b6feb4) — this control had no pros/cons source in the repo. The
    shortcomings are Jon's: the three ways a gateway fails in practice are
    coverage, availability and network plumbing, not cost. -#}
{{ _profile(
   "Gateways",
   "Every model call routed through one place you control",
   [{"title": "Sees every call, whatever made it",
     "detail": "The one control on this list with no bypass. Logging, redaction and model allowlists apply to every client at once."},
    {"title": "Decides inline, not after the fact",
     "detail": "Tens of milliseconds on the request path, so the call is shaped before it happens rather than audited afterwards."},
    {"title": "One place to change your mind",
     "detail": "Swap a model, tighten redaction, revoke a provider &mdash; once, for everyone, without touching a repository."}],
   [{"title": "Every client needs to be configured to use it",
     "detail": "Miss one IDE, one CI runner, one laptop, and that traffic never reaches the gateway at all."},
    {"title": "Single point of failure",
     "detail": "Everything routes through it. When it is down, every agent in the company is down with it."},
    {"title": "Requires specific network routing",
     "detail": "Egress rules, proxies and TLS interception all have to agree. That is a network project, not a config change."}],
   {"setup": 3, "maintenance": 3, "cost": 2, "speed": 3, "effectiveness": 4, "repeatable": 4},
   good_label="Benefits", avoid_label="Shortcomings") }}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro human_review() -%}
{#- The control every room already has, and the only one on the list that gets
    worse as volume goes up — which is the tension the whole talk is about.
    Both columns are Jon's. Note the hedges are load-bearing: "can catch novel
    issues" is qualified on experience, and "has more institutional context" is
    qualified on *today* — the claim is that this advantage is real and
    shrinking, not that it is permanent. -#}
{{ _profile(
   "Human review",
   "A person reading the change before it lands",
   [{"title": "Often more cautious",
     "detail": "A person who is unsure slows down and asks. Nothing else on this list hesitates."},
    {"title": "Can catch novel issues",
     "detail": "The failure nobody wrote a rule for and no model had seen &mdash; given a reviewer with the experience to recognise it."},
    {"title": "Has more institutional context",
     "detail": "Today. They know what this service is for, who depends on it, and what broke last time."}],
   [{"title": "Doesn&rsquo;t scale well",
     "detail": "Capacity does not grow with generated code. It becomes the bottleneck the moment agents start writing."},
    {"title": "Inconsistent quality",
     "detail": "Same diff, different verdict &mdash; depending on who is looking and how many they have already read this week."},
    {"title": "Rubber-stamping is invisible",
     "detail": "Approval after reading and approval at volume look identical in the record. Nothing distinguishes them afterwards."}],
   {"setup": 3, "maintenance": 3, "cost": 1, "speed": 1, "effectiveness": 3, "repeatable": 2},
   good_label="Benefits", avoid_label="Shortcomings") }}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro agent_review() -%}
{#- Broad coverage, real judgement, and non-deterministic — the profile is
    almost the inverse of hooks, which is why teams run both.
    Copy brought over from the "Deterministic + AI" comparison in
    modules/guardrails/layered-defense.j2, which is where this argument was
    already made well, and refitted to this slide's title-plus-sentence shape.
    "Non-deterministic" absorbs the audit consequence that used to be its own
    row ("Not evidence"), since three items is the shape here. -#}
{{ _profile(
   "Agent review",
   "A second model arguing with the first, on the diff",
   [{"title": "Reasons about intent and context",
     "detail": "Reads the diff like a teammate and understands the change, rather than matching it against a pattern."},
    {"title": "Catches novel issues",
     "detail": "Finds shapes no rule covers &mdash; including subtle logic flaws nobody thought to write a check for."},
    {"title": "Suggests fixes",
     "detail": "Actionable patches, not just flags, and it adapts them to your codebase."}],
   [{"title": "Non-deterministic",
     "detail": "The same diff may flag different things on different runs. &ldquo;An agent reviewed it&rdquo; satisfies no auditor."},
    {"title": "Slower, and costs money",
     "detail": "LLM compute adds latency to every review and a dollar cost to every pull request."},
    {"title": "Can hallucinate",
     "detail": "It may fabricate findings, or miss real ones &mdash; and it will do both fluently."}],
   {"setup": 4, "maintenance": 3, "cost": 2, "speed": 3, "effectiveness": 3, "repeatable": 2},
   good_label="Benefits", avoid_label="Shortcomings") }}
{%- endmacro -%}
